


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: schoning
@title: State of the Art: E-LAUTE – The New Edition of the Lute Tablatures
@presenter: schoning
@abstract: 

E-LAUTE (Electronic Linked Annotated Unified Tablature
Edition: (https://e-laute.info/;
https://edition.onb.ac.at/context:elaute/) is a research
project establishing an open-access, comprehensive, and
interactive edition of lute tablatures from the
German-speaking area from 1450–1550. This corpus –
consisting of some 2,000 pages – has become largely
inaccessible to scholars, musicians, and the general public
due to its unwieldy, complex notation. E-LAUTE is
sustainably digitising this vital part of European cultural
heritage as machine-readable music encodings. It unlocks
this repertoire for a contemporary audience through
transliteration into modernly understood types of lute
tablature, transcription into common Western music
notation, and professional audio recordings. Enhanced by
music-historical and performance-practical metadata, the
data is dynamically interlinked. The primary aim is to
create a novel form of music edition – an ‘open
knowledge platform’ intertwining musicology, music
practice, music informatics, and literary studies
– which will be permanently hosted by the Austrian
National Library (ÖNB) and integrated into the RISM
(Répertoire International des Sources Musicales) database.

This paper provides a concise overview of the current state
of the overall project, focussing on four core pillars of
its development: (i) the usability of the E-LAUTE-ÖNB
platform [2]; (ii) the project database, which contains
re-indexed sources, new text and music concordances,
historical contexts, and performance information; (iii) the
editorial process and our underlying policies; and (iv) the
technical project infrastructure. Furthermore, particular
emphasis is placed on the project’s latest challenges and
future-facing developments. These notably include the
implementation of knowledge graph structures, further
integration and synergies with RISM, and the exploration of
additional collaborative opportunities within the field of
digital musicology.


@@END: PRESENTATION



